[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known, with one fix queued. Its reports describe three
different bugs. The desk merged them because their subject lines matched
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and the subject says nothing about the cause.
The parser fix covers only the first one.

<Callout>
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. <code>parser</code> is fixed
(<code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>), and its ticket is closed. <code>hint</code> won't be
fixed because the booking-v3 form is retired, so its ticket is closed as not planned. The #4471 work
did not cover <code>one-day</code>, and its ticket is closed as not planned too. If a one-day move
comes in again, open a new ticket for it. Do not merge it into #4471.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

Counts are unique reports. The export repeats some of them across pages, so they are counted once by id.

## parser: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every product reads dates through one shared parser, and it reads month first. All of our tenants
write the day first, so any date where both numbers are 12 or less comes out with day and month
swapped: 2 December becomes 12 February. This comes in through the app, by email, and through
booking-v2 forms. It is what #4471 says it is.

## hint: booking-v3 prints the wrong format under its date field

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. People type what the hint tells them, month
first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The result looks the same as a parser
flip, but the bug is in the form. The parser fix leaves it as it is. Only booking-v3 tenants
get it, and only through their booking form. The desk's own table on #4471 already marks these
as *not this ticket*.

Intake tags do not help here. They are guessed from the reporter's browser, so an `en-US` browser
gets tagged `hint` wherever its tenant is. Go by tenant and channel ([triage](/kb/triage/dedupe)).

## one-day: the date moves by one day

These reports are not a month flip. The day moves by one: Tuesday shows as Monday; a client in
Lisbon and a client in Toronto get different days for the same meeting; an invite arrives before
the booking was made; bookings in one month are one day off. That points at time zones when the
invite is written, not at how a date is read. The desk noted them as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`). The #4471 table counts them under `parser` by tenant, so the parser fix
would leave them open.

## For the desk

- A big ticket can be several bugs that share a subject line. Before you mark one known, split it
  by cause, not by subject.
- When a cause gets a fix, close only the reports of that cause.
- Decision 0001 merges on subject alone. It needs a new record that merges on cause.

